# This is the generic template for any Postgres (a self-managed dev instance,
# a real deployment, ...). For local development against the Postgres this
# repo's docker-compose.yml provisions, use `.env.compose.example` instead —
# `cp .env.compose.example .env` works with no hand-editing; see
# docs/maintainers/local-development.md.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://avalon:<password>@<host>:5432/avalon
# Keep this bound to loopback even in a real deployment — put a reverse
# proxy terminating TLS in front of it, never expose this port directly.
# See docs/maintainers/deployment.md.
AVALON_SERVER_ADDR=127.0.0.1:8080

# Which network this process/database belongs to — e.g.
# `avalon-mainnet-1` for the real production ledger, `avalon-dev-<yourname>`
# for a local/dev one. Committed into the ledger's genesis on first boot
# against an empty database, and checked against it on every boot after
# that — a mismatch is fatal, by design, so a dev process can never be
# accidentally pointed at a production database (or vice versa) and start
# writing to it. Never share a network_id between a real deployment and any
# dev/test database.
AVALON_NETWORK_ID=avalon-dev-local

# WebAuthn relying-party identity. rp_id must be an effective
# domain of the origin below — use `localhost`, not `127.0.0.1`: WebAuthn's
# browser secure-context exception is specifically for the hostname
# `localhost`. The origin must match the Hub's actual dev server origin
# exactly, including port — webauthn-rs validates it byte-for-byte against
# what the browser reports, and the Hub serves from Vite's
# dev port, not avalon-server's own port.
AVALON_WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=localhost
AVALON_WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# Browser origin(s) allowed to call this API cross-origin — comma-separated
# if more than one. Defaults to the Hub's Vite dev
# port if unset; only needs setting explicitly for a non-default port or a
# real deployment origin.
AVALON_HUB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# Settlement operator signing key — Signed Tree Heads only, no per-entry
# signatures. A raw
# 32-byte Ed25519 seed, hex-encoded (64 hex characters). Required by
# whatever process actually commits batches (avalon-server's settlement
# worker); never stored in Postgres. Generate a throwaway dev value with:
#   python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
# AVALON_SETTLEMENT_SIGNING_KEY=<64 hex characters>

# Optional label distinguishing this key from a future rotated one — every
# Signed Tree Head records whichever value was active when it was signed.
# Defaults to "settlement-operator-1" if unset.
# AVALON_SETTLEMENT_SIGNING_KEY_ID=settlement-operator-1

# Social recovery's mandatory public time-delay: once a recovery request
# collects enough guardian
# approvals to meet its threshold, this many hours must pass — with no
# owner/guardian veto — before the requesting device's passkey becomes
# valid. Defaults to 48 if unset or non-positive; the ticket suggests
# 24-72h as a reasonable range, long enough for an infrequently-online
# owner to plausibly notice, short enough that a genuine all-devices-lost
# recovery doesn't drag on for a week.
# AVALON_RECOVERY_DELAY_HOURS=48

# The write-time abuse floor on attestation issuance: how many attestations
# one issuer may
# write about one subject within the rolling window before further
# writes are rejected (429 ATTESTATION_WRITE_QUOTA_EXCEEDED), and how
# many hours that window covers. Defaults to a generous 500/1h if unset
# or non-positive — a floor against runaway/abusive volume, never a
# throughput target. Set both low (e.g. 3, 1) to exercise the rejection
# path in crates/server/tests/achievement_write_quota.rs without issuing
# hundreds of real attestations.
# AVALON_ACHIEVEMENT_WRITE_QUOTA=500
# AVALON_ACHIEVEMENT_WRITE_QUOTA_WINDOW_HOURS=1

# The public half of AVALON_SETTLEMENT_SIGNING_KEY, hex-encoded — all
# `avalon inspect-ledger` or a read-only mirror ever needs to verify a
# Signed Tree Head; never the private key. Derived automatically from
# AVALON_SETTLEMENT_SIGNING_KEY when unset, as a single-operator dev
# convenience — set this explicitly instead in any setting where the
# verifier shouldn't have the private key available at all.
#
# This is also the value that goes into docs/trusted-networks.json's
# `verify_key` for whatever AVALON_NETWORK_ID this deployment runs, so
# clients (the Hub, eventually the SDK) can pin this specific network
# instead of trusting AVALON_NETWORK_ID's string alone — see
# docs/architecture/network-trust-anchors.md.
# AVALON_SETTLEMENT_VERIFY_KEY=<64 hex characters>

# Node-tiered durable history retention — see docs/architecture/nodes.md's
# "Settlement retention tiers" section. Declares which retention tier this
# node runs as; never affects
# the hash-chain/Merkle commitment itself, only local availability of raw
# event bodies (ledger_entries.payload). Defaults to "full" (complete
# history, nothing ever pruned) if unset.
# AVALON_RETENTION_TIER=full

# Mirror-watcher (no-consensus mirror model — see
# crates/server/src/mirror_watcher.rs and
# docs/architecture/settlement.md's "Mirror-watcher" section). Comma-
# separated base URLs of peers to watch (e.g.
# "http://localhost:8081,http://localhost:8082") — unset or empty means
# this node isn't watching anyone, and the background worker isn't even
# spawned.
#
# Each peer is verified against the pinned verify_key for
# whatever network_id its own STH claims, looked up in
# docs/trusted-networks.json — never against this node's own
# AVALON_SETTLEMENT_VERIFY_KEY/SIGNING_KEY (that variable no longer has any
# effect on mirroring). A peer claiming a network_id with no trust-anchor
# entry there is refused outright, not silently mirrored under its own
# name. A node can mirror peers on more than one legitimately pinned
# network at once, each verified against its own correct key. To mirror a
# network without a real published deployment yet (a private/local trust
# anchor), add an entry to docs/trusted-networks.json rather than trying to
# point AVALON_SETTLEMENT_VERIFY_KEY at it.
# AVALON_MIRROR_PEERS=http://localhost:8081

# How often the mirror-watcher polls each configured peer's latest STH.
# An STH is a few hundred bytes of JSON and this is node-to-node traffic
# only (never end-user clients), so bandwidth isn't the constraint —
# 30-60s is a sensible cadence. Defaults to 30 if unset.
# AVALON_MIRROR_POLL_INTERVAL_SECS=30

# Node-to-node announce/bootstrap discovery — see crates/server/src/nodes.rs and
# docs/architecture/nodes.md's "Discovery" section). Comma-separated base
# URLs to announce to. Optional: unset falls back to this network's
# `seed_nodes` in docs/trusted-networks.json — empty for a network with no
# anchor node yet, which is expected for a network's first node, not an
# error.
# AVALON_BOOTSTRAP_PEERS=http://localhost:8081

# This node's own externally-reachable base URL — required to announce
# itself to any bootstrap/seed peer (a peer needs a real URL to reach this
# node back at). Unset means this node can still be announced TO via
# POST /nodes/announce, it just won't announce itself anywhere.
# AVALON_NODE_URL=http://localhost:8080

# How often the announce worker re-announces to each configured/seeded
# peer, and the unit a peer is pruned from the local peer table after (a
# few missed intervals). Defaults to 180 if unset.
# AVALON_ANNOUNCE_INTERVAL_SECS=180

# Comma-separated capabilities this node advertises when announcing
# (matching docs/architecture/nodes.md's Settlement/Indexer/Realtime/
# Gateway names). Defaults to "combined" — milestone 1's "one avalon-server
# process" reality. Excluding "realtime" from this list is now
# load-bearing, not just advisory — see AVALON_REALTIME_URL below.
# AVALON_NODE_ROLES=combined

# Required when AVALON_NODE_ROLES excludes "realtime" — the
# base URL of a separately-running avalon-server process holding the
# realtime role, that this node proxies every /ws/presence and
# /ws/messages WebSocket connection through to instead of handling them
# locally. See crate::realtime_proxy and docs/architecture/nodes.md's
# "Today in the repo" section. Unset (and "realtime" excluded from
# AVALON_NODE_ROLES) refuses to start rather than silently serving
# WebSocket connections locally anyway.
# AVALON_REALTIME_URL=http://localhost:8098

# Required when AVALON_NODE_ROLES excludes "indexer" (and isn't
# "combined") — the base URL of a separately-running avalon-server process
# holding the indexer role, that this node routes every indexer read/write
# to over a node-to-node RPC protocol instead of running its own
# local PostgresIndexer. See crate::internal_role::RemoteIndexer and
# docs/architecture/nodes.md's "Backing-service discovery for a Gateway
# process" section. Unset (and "indexer" excluded from
# AVALON_NODE_ROLES) refuses to start, same posture AVALON_REALTIME_URL
# above takes.
# AVALON_INDEXER_REMOTE_URL=http://localhost:8097

# Shared-secret bearer credential for the /internal/* node-to-
# node RPC protocol (today: crate::internal_role's Indexer apply/rebuild
# endpoints) — set identically on every process in one operator's own
# deployment that calls, or is called over, this protocol. Its own separate
# key domain from AVALON_SETTLEMENT_SUBMIT_KEY/AVALON_ADMIN_TOKEN — see
# crate::internal_role's module doc comment for why. Unset means every
# request under /internal/* is refused, never silently open.
# AVALON_INTERNAL_ROLE_KEY=<a long random shared secret>

# A Gateway process configured with a remote Indexer/Realtime/
# Settlement backing-service URL (the three vars above, plus
# AVALON_SETTLEMENT_REMOTE_URL(S) further below) now also probes each one's
# actual reachability at startup (GET /nodes/status) — logged as a warning,
# never a startup failure, if it can't be reached (a backing service briefly
# down mid rolling-restart shouldn't block this process from starting). See
# docs/architecture/nodes.md's "Backing-service discovery for a Gateway
# process" section for the full writeup; no env var of its own, this
# note just points at where the behavior lives.

# Can only RAISE the effective minimum peer protocol version
# above the binary's own baked-in MIN_SUPPORTED_PEER_VERSION floor, never
# lower it — see crates/server/src/version.rs and
# docs/architecture/nodes.md's "Version rollout" section. An unparseable
# value is ignored (falls back to the baked-in floor), not a startup error.
# AVALON_MIN_PEER_VERSION=0.1.0

# This node's libp2p Kademlia DHT swarm — see
# crates/server/src/dht.rs and docs/architecture/nodes.md's "Discovery"
# section. On by default (an opt-out escape hatch, not an
# opt-in gate) — set to "false" or "0" for a single-node/private
# self-hoster who wants zero DHT overhead, or to roll back if a real
# problem surfaces while this is still young, unproven-at-real-scale code.
# AVALON_DHT_ENABLED=false

# This node's libp2p identity keypair — a raw 32-byte Ed25519 seed,
# hex-encoded, same convention as AVALON_SETTLEMENT_SIGNING_KEY above but a
# completely separate key domain (see crate::dht's module doc for why).
# Unset generates a fresh identity every restart, which is fine for local
# dev but means peers need to relearn this node's PeerId each time; set
# this for a stable identity in any longer-lived deployment.
# AVALON_LIBP2P_IDENTITY_KEY=<64 hex characters>

# The multiaddr this node's DHT swarm binds. Defaults to
# /ip4/0.0.0.0/tcp/0 (an ephemeral port on every interface) when unset.
# AVALON_LIBP2P_LISTEN_ADDR=/ip4/0.0.0.0/tcp/4001

# The multiaddr other peers should actually dial to reach this node's DHT
# swarm — set this for any containerized/NATed deployment (see
# docker-compose.yml's avalon-server service and crate::dht's module doc):
# a container only ever observes its own internal bridge/loopback address
# on its own, the same reason AVALON_NODE_URL exists for the HTTP peer
# table below. Unset (fine for native, non-containerized dev) announces
# whatever address the DHT swarm actually observes itself listening on.
# AVALON_LIBP2P_EXTERNAL_ADDR=/ip4/203.0.113.10/tcp/4001

# How often this node re-scans its HTTP peer table (crate::nodes) for a
# DHT identity it hasn't dialed into the kad swarm yet. Defaults to 30 if
# unset/invalid. Mainly useful to turn down for local testing (see
# crates/server/tests/interest_dht.rs) — a real deployment has little
# reason to change this from the default.
# AVALON_DHT_BOOTSTRAP_SCAN_INTERVAL_SECS=30

# Required when AVALON_RETENTION_TIER=hot: how many days of committed
# history this node keeps payloads for. Purely a declared config value —
# on its own it does NOT delete anything; see AVALON_RETENTION_PRUNING_ENABLED.
# AVALON_RETENTION_HOT_WINDOW_DAYS=180

# The actual opt-in that lets a hot-tier node delete anything. Off by
# default, and deliberately a second, separate flag from the tier itself —
# a node can declare "hot" and still retain everything until an operator
# explicitly flips this. IMPORTANT: milestone 1 runs exactly one settlement
# database (see docs/architecture/settlement.md's "Today in the repo"
# section) — there is no other node or mirror holding a second copy of
# anything yet. Setting this to true today means payloads older than the
# window are PERMANENTLY AND IRRECOVERABLY LOST the moment they're pruned,
# not "still available from an archive node elsewhere" — that guarantee
# doesn't exist yet at this milestone. Only enable this once a real
# archive-tier mirror (or equivalent replication) is actually running.
# AVALON_RETENTION_PRUNING_ENABLED=false

# Remote-settlement mode — see docs/architecture/nodes.md's "the first real instance of
# 'a node without its own Settlement'" section). When set, this node's
# outbox worker stops committing batches locally and instead POSTs each one
# to /ledger/submit on the named Settlement authority, which runs the exact
# same commit call for it that it runs for its own local outbox. Unset (the
# default), nothing about this node's behavior changes. Base URL, no
# trailing slash — e.g. "http://localhost:8081". Requires
# AVALON_SETTLEMENT_SUBMIT_KEY to actually be accepted by the authority,
# and (for this node's own reads to eventually reflect what it submits)
# AVALON_MIRROR_PEERS pointed at that same authority.
# AVALON_SETTLEMENT_REMOTE_URL=http://localhost:8081

# The per-shard generalization of the singular var above, once
# sharded settlement is in use — a comma-separated shard_id=url
# list, e.g. "game:ashen-realms=https://a.example,core=https://core.example".
# A shard with no entry here is committed locally by this node. The
# singular AVALON_SETTLEMENT_REMOTE_URL above keeps working unchanged — it
# merges in as this map's implicit "core=<url>" entry whenever this var
# doesn't already name "core" itself. Unset (the default, milestone-1's
# single-shard reality), nothing changes.
# AVALON_SETTLEMENT_REMOTE_URLS=game:ashen-realms=http://localhost:8082,core=http://localhost:8081

# Shared-secret bearer credential for POST /ledger/submit — the
# node-to-node write endpoint a remote-settlement node's outbox worker calls
# above. Must be set identically on both the submitting node (sent as
# `Authorization: Bearer <key>`) and the authority receiving it (checked
# against the same value). An authority with this unset refuses every
# submission outright rather than accepting one unauthenticated.
# AVALON_SETTLEMENT_SUBMIT_KEY=<a long random shared secret>

# Managed settlement hosting. A managed host runs
# POST /ledger/prepare-batch / POST /ledger/finalize-batch (also gated on
# AVALON_SETTLEMENT_SUBMIT_KEY above, same shared-secret mechanism) so an
# integrator can get a real, authoritative shard without running
# avalon-server themselves — the integrator's own settlement signing key
# never leaves their control or reaches this node; only a signature does.
# This is the hosted integrator's *public* key, hex-encoded, used only to
# verify a finalize-batch caller's signature. Interim/single-key-per-node
# (see docs/architecture/self-hosting.md's "Managed hosting" section) —
# unset, both endpoints refuse every request.
# AVALON_MANAGED_HOSTING_VERIFY_KEY=<hex-encoded 32-byte Ed25519 public key>

# Cross-shard root aggregation. GET /ledger/cross-shard-root
# fetches, verifies, and aggregates every named shard's current STH into
# one deterministic root anyone can independently recompute — see
# docs/architecture/settlement.md's "Cross-shard commitment" section.
# Comma-separated shard_id=url list, e.g.
# "game:ashen-realms=https://a.example,core=https://core.example". Unset
# (the default): falls back to this node's own local STH as the one-shard
# degenerate case, no network calls.
# AVALON_KNOWN_SHARDS=core=http://localhost:8080
# Fallback per-shard verify keys (comma-separated shard_id=hexkey) — the
# real mechanism (an integrator's registered shard_settlement-
# purpose issuer key, POST /integrations/{slug}/keys) is checked first;
# this only matters for a shard with no such key resolved (e.g. "core",
# which has no owning integrator). A shard named in AVALON_KNOWN_SHARDS
# with no key resolved from either source is treated as missing.
# AVALON_SHARD_VERIFY_KEYS=core=<hex-encoded 32-byte Ed25519 public key>

# Hoster-configurable resource limits. Every one defaults to exactly what this process
# hardcoded before this ticket — an unconfigured node behaves exactly as
# it always has, never "unlimited" and never "fails to start".

# Postgres connection pool size (crates/server/src/main.rs). Defaults to 10.
# AVALON_MAX_DB_CONNECTIONS=10

# Ceiling on requests handled concurrently (tower::limit::ConcurrencyLimitLayer).
# Requests past this backpressure (bounded wait), they are never dropped
# without a response. Defaults to 256.
# AVALON_MAX_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS=256

# Rate limit, per caller, per minute (tower_governor, GCRA-based). Keyed by
# the caller's x-avalon-integrator-key-id header when present, falling
# back to source IP for pre-auth endpoints (registration, login). Requests
# past this get 429 with a Retry-After header — never a silent drop or a
# generic 500. Defaults to 600.
# AVALON_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=600

# When set, both limits above are enforced via this Redis
# instance instead of per-process memory — shared across every
# avalon-server process YOU run that points at the same Redis (never
# shared across different hosters/operators; this is your own Redis for
# your own deployment, the same way DATABASE_URL is your own Postgres).
# Unset (the default): both limits stay exactly the per-process behavior
# they've always been — no new infra required.
# See docs/architecture/nodes.md's "Today in the repo" entry for the details.
# This same Redis is also reused (when AVALON_DHT_ENABLED is also
# set) as a local fast-path in front of interest-scoped realtime relay
# lookups — never required for correctness, purely a same-fleet latency
# shortcut in front of the real DHT lookup. See crate::interest::RedisFastPath's
# own module doc comment.
# AVALON_REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379

# The outbox worker's drain poll cadence, in seconds
# (crates/server/src/outbox.rs::run_worker). Defaults to 3.
# AVALON_OUTBOX_POLL_INTERVAL_SECS=3

# Shared-secret bearer credential for GET/POST /nodes/log-level,
# letting you bump or lower this process's own log verbosity at runtime
# (RUST_LOG-style filter syntax) without a restart. Deliberately a separate
# secret from AVALON_SETTLEMENT_SUBMIT_KEY above (see crate::admin's module
# doc comment for why) — unset means both endpoints refuse every request,
# same posture every other shared-secret-gated endpoint in this file takes.
# AVALON_ADMIN_TOKEN=<a long random shared secret>

